  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nWe'll follow up Saturday's gorgeous start to August with another \nsuperb day of Summer weather ahead! All is quiet early this morning, \nwith light winds and clear skies over the region. We're watching\nsurface observations in the typical ground fog prone locations,\nbut so far there has been little in the way of visibility \nreductions. Short term guidance is most aggressive with fog \nalong the MN River in western MN, but current trends would \nsuggest it will be a few more hours before greater coverage of \nfog develops. Fog will once again erode shortly after daybreak \nand set the stage for a beautiful Sunday with highs in the low \nto mid 80s under sunny skies. Southeasterly winds may become \nbreezy at times across western MN as the surface pressure \ngradient begins to tighten ahead of an incoming weather system \nfrom the northwest.\n\nThe Upper Midwest remains beneath a ridge that extends from the Four \nCorners region all the way into western Ontario. The ridge axis is \nprogged to translate east over the next few days as an active \nPacific jet (currently coming ashore over the Pacific northwest) \nspreads across the northern CONUS. A vigorous shortwave will \nadvance through central Canada Monday, driving a trailing cold front \neastward across the Dakotas. The front will spawn the highest \nconvective chances during the entire forecast period Monday evening \nacross west-central MN. SPC's new Day 2 convective outlook features \nlittle change from the prior Day 3, with a Slight Risk (Level 2/5)\nin place across the far northwestern extent of the forecast area\nand up into the Red River Valley. A Marginal Risk (Level 1/5) \nextends southeast of the Slight Risk to a line from Redwood \nCounty to Mille Lacs County. The setup for severe storms is \ndriven by a secondary embedded shortwave that will kick through \nthe broad upper flow Monday evening and support deeper convection\nacross the eastern Dakotas. Forecast soundings across western \nMN highlight stout capping as an EML expands east over the \nregion Monday afternoon. This should work to limit any early \nattempts at convective initiation despite the eastward expansion\nof stronger flow aloft. The aforementioned frontal forcing and \na strengthening low-level jet will support a linear complex of \nstorms moving into western portions of the forecast area later \nMonday evening. Damaging winds and hail are primary threats \nowing to a pool of ~1500 J/kg of MLCAPE and 35-40kts of \neffective shear per the latest HREF & REFS guidance. Convection\nwill move east through the night and likely weaken given the \nunfavorable nocturnal timing, along with displacement from the\nstronger synoptic forcing to the northwest.\n\nA few showers or a storm may linger into Tuesday morning prior to \nthe passage of the cold front. It's possible that a few showers or \nstorm may fire along the front near the MN/IA border in the \nafternoon, but those chances should be relatively short-lived. \nWe'll keep chance PoPs from the NBM in the grids along I-90 for \nnow and will lean on forthcoming CAM ensembles to better define \nthe PoP window. Otherwise, Tuesday looks like a quiet day of \nweather for most of the forecast area with northwesterly flow \nand mid-level cold advection behind the frontal passage. Cooler \nhigh pressure settles in mid-week and will result high\ntemperatures falling slightly below normal (upper 70s) Tuesday \n& Wednesday. Speaking of cooler temperatures, overnight lows are\nforecast to dip into the lower 50s for locations outside of the\nTwin Cities urban heat island Wednesday morning. Warmer mid- \nlevel temperatures sourced from ~600 dm Four Corners ridge are \nforecast to expand east later this week, which results in \ntemperatures climbing back into the mid 80s late week into the \nweekend. Precipitation chances look isolated at best through the\nsecond half of the forecast period.",
